## 3. CORRECTION ENGINE / DICTIONARY

| # | Edge case | Why it matters | Expected behavior |
|---|---|---|---|
| 3.1 | Multi-word phrase correction ("play show" → "Play Store") | Confirmed working (`applyCorrectionsToUtterance` matches phrases), but re-verify after any refactor | Longest-match-first phrase correction still applies |
| 3.2 | Two corrections that overlap/conflict (e.g. "play show" and "show" both have rules) | Untested | Deterministic precedence, no silent double-application |
| 3.3 | Correction dictionary at scale (100+ entries) — performance | Untested | No meaningful latency added to dictation with a large dictionary |
| 3.4 | "no no" course-correction combined with a name correction in the same utterance | Untested combination of two working features | Both apply correctly, no interference |
| 3.5 | User corrects a word, then says a DIFFERENT word that's phonetically similar | Untested | Correct word wins; system doesn't over-correct to the wrong dictionary entry |
| 3.6 | Dictionary entry with special characters/unicode names (e.g. "Zoë", "Núñez") | Untested | Stored and matched correctly, no encoding corruption |

